Importing an Image
The Pattern Editor can also import external images in formats such as:

PNG;
JPEG;
BMP;
WebP.

The imported image is automatically:

converted to RGB;
resized proportionally to 48 pixels wide;
reduced to three colours;
converted into an editable Single Pattern.

The aspect ratio is preserved when calculating the new height.
If the resulting image exceeds the maximum height of 1000 pixels, the import is rejected.
An image that cannot be reduced successfully to exactly three colours is also rejected.

Exporting the Pattern
When Save Pattern PNG is selected, the editor first verifies that:

the final pattern exists;
its width is 48 pixels;
its height does not exceed 1000 pixels;
exactly three colours are used.

The file is then exported as an RGB PNG.
After saving, the editor displays:

the final image dimensions;
the association between the three colours and commands C1, C2 and C3.

The next developments for the vision subsystem are:

multi-frame decision fusion;
learned feature representations, such as lightweight CNNs;
improved mechanical stability and camera alignment;
integration of the detected alarm with the real ESP32/machine control workflow.

The operator interface is implemented with a Pimoroni GFX HAT connected to the Raspberry Pi. The board provides:

a 128 × 64 monochrome LCD;
six capacitive touch buttons;
RGB backlighting;
six button indicator LEDs.

The interface is controlled by the integrated script:
raspberry.py
This script no longer acts only as a display demonstration: in real mode it selects the PNG pattern, opens the USB serial port to the ESP32, sends colour commands and updates progress when the ESP32 reports a microswitch event.

