7. Prepare the Hardware
Before starting a real test, verify that:

the Raspberry Pi is powered;
the GFX HAT display is connected to the Raspberry Pi;
the ESP32 is connected to the Raspberry Pi through its USB cable;
the three servomotors and the stepper are supplied by the external power supply;
all the GND are connected;
the microswitch is connected to the ESP32.

See Wiring and Power Supply for the complete connections.

12. Start Production
Press PLUS (AVVIA).
The Raspberry Pi:

validates the PNG file;
checks that the image is 48 pixels wide;
checks that the image contains exactly three RGB colours;
opens the USB serial communication with the ESP32;
sends the command corresponding to the first pixel.

The Raspberry Pi implements the high-level control layer of the prototype. It is responsible for:

displaying the operator interface through the GFX HAT;
reading PNG files from the USB flash drive;
copying the selected pattern to local storage;
validating the PNG dimensions and number of colours;
mapping RGB colours to the commands C1, C2 and C3;
communicating with the ESP32 through USB serial;
displaying the current processing progress;
hosting the error-recognition pipeline.

